<ul>
<li><strong>Greedy</strong>: sort by some key, walk once, commit. Usually O(n log n) because of the sort. Its risk is being wrong — it must be proved.</li>
<li><strong>Divide and conquer</strong>: the subproblems do not overlap, so nothing needs to be remembered; the work is in the combine step.</li>
<li><strong>DP</strong> (decks ⭐ CS.3–CS.4): subproblems overlap (Fibonacci in lesson 3.3), so results are stored in a table.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Question to ask</th><th>Yes ⇒</th></tr></thead>
